<!-- Aura-SOC - rule 100943 (level 12). Domain: suricata,ids,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="suricata,ids,">

  <!-- A high-severity signature seen INSIDE the network.

       Suricata grades its own signatures: severity 1 = high, 2 = medium,
       3 = informational. Severity 1 is rare here - 13 events in a 20 000-line
       sample on 2026-08-11 - which makes it usable as-is, provided the
       perimeter is excluded (see 100941: 11 of those 13 were internet scans
       hitting the WAN).

       Interface mapping on home-r-pf01, checked 2026-08-11:
         vtnet0 = WAN/LAN100   vtnet2 = CONT200   vtnet3 = ADMIN300
         vtnet4 = LAB500       vtnet5 = DMZ600

       On vtnet2-5 the traffic is between machines we own. A high-severity
       signature there means either a compromised host attacking its neighbours
       (lateral movement) or something that already got through the firewall -
       both worth a case, hence level 12.

       Exploit and web-attack families are included at the same level on those
       segments only: an exploit attempt from the internet is background noise,
       the same attempt from CONT200 towards ADMIN300 is an incident. -->

  <rule id="100943" level="12">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="in_iface" type="pcre2">^vtnet[2-5]$</field>
    <field name="alert.severity" type="pcre2">^1$</field>
    <description>Suricata: high-severity signature on an internal segment ($(in_iface)) - $(alert.signature) [$(src_ip) -> $(dest_ip)]</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1210</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>lateral_movement,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
